From daniel.dvorkin at gmail.com  Tue Feb 26 02:29:01 2008
From: daniel.dvorkin at gmail.com (Daniel Dvorkin)
Date: Mon, 25 Feb 2008 19:29:01 -0700
Subject: Re: [Rpy] RPy, data frames, and referencing columns
In-Reply-To: <47C37092.3030006@stamlab.org>
References: <47C37092.3030006@stamlab.org>
Message-ID: <95594ED6-24BA-4830-9203-9D8BD7534D8F@gmail.com>

It looks like a conversion mode problem.  "df" in your example is  
converted back into a Python dict as soon as it's created, so when  
you pass it to r.is_data_frame(), R doesn't know what it is.  Try the  
following:

df = with_mode(NO_CONVERSION, r.data_frame) \
              (timeDataFrame["utility"], \
               timeDataFrame["target"], \
               timeDataFrame["build"], \
               timeDataFrame["timeType"], \
               timeDataFrame["time"])
r.print_(r.is_data_frame(df))

